  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nA weak cold frontal passage is expected tonight, but the lack of\nmoisture will help us maintain a dry forecast. Surface high pressure\nwill hold influence over our weather pattern through the remainder of\nthe work week, keeping fair weather conditions in place across the \nOhio Valley. Temperatures will be a couple degrees cooler on\nThursday/Friday compared to today, but still remain above seasonal\nnormals. Temperatures will continue to climb on Saturday ahead of \nthe TS remnants, resulting in highs in the 80s for most.\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nTropical Storm Isaias is expected to strengthen into a hurricane\nWednesday night into Thursday. The remnants from this TS are most\nlikely to reach our fa late Saturday into Sunday. As the system\napproaches Saturday evening, breezy conditions are forecast to\ndevelop. As of now, model guidance is suggesting that wind gusts of\n25-35 MPH are possible Saturday evening into Saturday night. As the\ncolumn of air continues to saturate overnight, these winds appear to\nsubside a bit. Widespread showers are expected to develop with this \nsystem, but rainfall amounts will be determined by the overall storm\ntrack. Currently, model/ensemble averages are around 0.5\" to 1.0\", \nwith 'high-end' amounts near 2.0\". Keep in mind that these values may\nbe in flux depending on the track of the system.",
